Now I can see the vulnerability. In `md_process_table_row`:

```c
n = ctx->n_table_cell_boundaries + 2;
pipe_offs = (OFF*) malloc(n * sizeof(OFF));
...
j = 0;
pipe_offs[j++] = beg;
for(i = TABLECELLBOUNDARIES.head; i >= 0; i = ctx->marks[i].next) {
    MD_MARK* mark = &ctx->marks[i];
    pipe_offs[j++] = mark->end;
}
pipe_offs[j++] = end+1;
```

The buffer is allocated with `n = ctx->n_table_cell_boundaries + 2` entries. But then it writes `beg`, one entry per cell boundary, and `end+1`. So it writes `n_table_cell_boundaries + 2` entries total, which matches the allocation. That seems fine...

Wait, but `ctx->n_table_cell_boundaries` is a global counter that accumulates across multiple table rows! Let me check where it's reset. Let me look at `md_analyze_table_cell_boundary` and where `n_table_cell_boundaries` is reset.

